HARTMESH_TENANT=example-tenant
HARTMESH_PUBLIC_HOST=tenant.example.com
HARTMESH_TRUSTED_PROXIES=192.0.2.10,192.0.2.11
HARTMESH_LISTEN=0.0.0.0:2026
HARTMESH_DATA_DIR=/srv/hartmesh
SANDBOX_RUNTIME=runsc
SANDBOX_EGRESS=allowlist
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=example-postgres-password
REDIS_PASSWORD=example-redis-password
AUTH_JWT_SECRET=example-session-signing-secret
# Sign-in mode (README: "Sign-in"). This example shows sign-on-only: the three keys below make the identity provider the one way in, with the callback https://<HARTMESH_PUBLIC_HOST>/api/v1/auth/callback/sso. Local passwords instead: remove them and set HARTMESH_LOCAL_PASSWORDS=allowed and HARTMESH_LOCAL_REGISTRATION=closed (an administrator adds each person) or =open (anyone may sign up). Neither, both, one or two of the three, or local passwords without the registration key refuses to start.
HARTMESH_SIGN_ON_ISSUER=https://login.example.com/realms/tenant
HARTMESH_SIGN_ON_CLIENT_ID=hartmesh
HARTMESH_SIGN_ON_CLIENT_SECRET=example-sign-on-client-secret
# Optional, sign-on only (README: "Membership follows the claim"): admission follows one claim of the token, named literally, with the comma-separated values that admit; HARTMESH_SIGN_ON_ROLES=<value>=admin,<value>=user makes the role follow it too (then HARTMESH_SIGN_ON_ADMINS must be unset). Absent, admission and roles are as before.
#HARTMESH_SIGN_ON_ACCESS_CLAIM=urn:example:workspace:roles
#HARTMESH_SIGN_ON_ACCESS_VALUES=admin,member
#HARTMESH_SIGN_ON_ROLES=admin=admin,member=user
# Optional, sign-on only: how far this VM's clock may disagree with the provider's on an ID token's timestamps, whole seconds 0 to 300. Absent, 60 -- enough for a guest between NTP polls, which with no tolerance refuses every sign-in by everyone until the clock catches up.
#HARTMESH_SIGN_ON_CLOCK_SKEW=60
# Optional (README: "Optional keys"): what the sign-in page, the browser tab, the workspace without a company name and the assistant call the product; one line, at most 40 characters. Absent, HartMesh.
#HARTMESH_PRODUCT_NAME=HartMesh
# Optional (README: "Storage and agents"): what the first start reserves on the data disk, every Space fully allocated.
# Needed = USERS x USER_GIB + USERS x ASSISTANTS_PER_USER x ASSISTANT_GIB + EMPLOYEES x EMPLOYEE_GIB + COMPANY_GIB,
# and the larger of 4 GiB and 10% of the data disk must stay free. The defaults need 31 GiB plus 5 GiB free on a 50 GiB disk.
#HARTMESH_USERS=10
#HARTMESH_USER_STORAGE_GIB=1
#HARTMESH_AI_ASSISTANTS_PER_USER=1
#HARTMESH_AI_ASSISTANT_STORAGE_GIB=1
#HARTMESH_AI_EMPLOYEES=10
#HARTMESH_AI_EMPLOYEE_STORAGE_GIB=1
#HARTMESH_COMPANY_STORAGE_GIB=1
# Optional (README: "Telegram bots"): each person may connect Telegram bots of their own; on when absent. `off` stops every bot and keeps every bot link and token; write it before the first start of a copy that will run beside its original.
#HARTMESH_TELEGRAM=on
# Optional (README: "Telegram bots"): the service that turns voice notes into text (auto, openai, gemini, off), and how many bots the deployment (1 to 500) and one person (1 to 40) may hold. Absent: auto, 100, 10.
#HARTMESH_TELEGRAM_VOICE=auto
#HARTMESH_TELEGRAM_MAX_BOTS=100
#HARTMESH_TELEGRAM_MAX_BOTS_PER_PERSON=10
# Provider keys the operator seeded for this tenant follow verbatim, any subset (possibly one) of the *_API_KEY names config.example.yaml references, e.g. OPENAI_API_KEY=..., ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=..., GEMINI_API_KEY=..., TAVILY_API_KEY=...; a key an administrator later sets in the product outranks the one here for its provider (README: "Provider keys in the product").
